# Verify the task independently

Act as an independent tester and reviewer who did not write the change: assume nothing works until
you have evidence. Prove on committed code that every in-scope requirement is met and every change
maps to one. Your verdict sends the task to Ship (`NEXT`) or back to the step that must act
(`FAILED`, `REQUIREMENTS_GAP`, `DESIGN_GAP`). You never fix anything yourself.

## Inputs

- The brief (see the Task brief section): the header decisions and accepted shas; objectives
  (`OBJ-n`) in `## Classification`; `## Quality attribute screening`; `## Change requests`;
  `## Implementation` (per-ID checklist and declared change map: claims to check, not evidence);
  the latest `## Rework` entry and earlier rounds of `## Dev QA report`. The original request is
  context only.
- For every feature in the header: `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/<feature>/{{vars.requirementsFile}}`
  and `{{vars.techDesignFile}}`, plus the baseline `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/{{vars.techDesignFile}}`,
  the index and `GLOSSARY.md`.
- The task branch at HEAD (committed code only), the diff base (step 2), and any code, test or
  history you need, unchanged code included.
- Text after the command: focus areas, or the developer's independence override.

## Procedure

1. **Independence.** If this conversation wrote or changed the task's code, stop: tell the user to
   run `{{command step.skillName}}` in a new session, ideally with another agent or model. Continue only on
   an explicit override, recorded in the report. Evidence is only what you read or run yourself;
   the brief's Implementation section, commit messages and summaries are claims. If your agent can
   start an independent reviewer you may use one for the code review, but nothing depends on it.
2. **Clean tree, diff base.** `git status` must be clean: uncommitted task work gets committed by
   the developer or implementation first; never touch unrelated changes without asking. Run
   `git fetch origin {{vars.mainBranch}}`, then take the first base that applies and record it:

   | Situation | Diff base |
   |---|---|
   | Task branch, `origin/{{vars.mainBranch}}` exists | `git merge-base HEAD origin/{{vars.mainBranch}}` |
   | Task branch, only a local `{{vars.mainBranch}}` | `git merge-base HEAD {{vars.mainBranch}}` |
   | Work sits on `{{vars.mainBranch}}` itself | the `Base:` commit of the brief header |
   | No commit before the task | the empty tree, `git hash-object -t tree /dev/null` |

   Material: `git diff --stat <base> HEAD`, `git diff <base> HEAD`, `git log --oneline <base>..`
   (empty tree: `git log --oneline`).
3. **Run the commands.** Run the test and check commands (Project commands) at HEAD; record passed,
   failed and skipped per command; rerun a flaky test several times. For each failure run the same
   command at the base in a separate worktree: `git worktree add ../{{project.name}}-baseline <base>`,
   install dependencies, run, `git worktree remove ../{{project.name}}-baseline`. Failing at the
   base too = pre-existing (a follow-up); new = blocker. No baseline possible (empty tree, no suite
   or command at the base): every failure counts; the report says "baseline: not available".
4. **Requirement mapping and tests coverage** (boxes 1 and 4 of the
   [QA checks](references/qa-checks.md)). Build the table ID | evidence | result:
   - every in-scope scenario ID is implemented at HEAD (read the code; for clarified tasks and
     extracted IDs, usually existing code) and has a passing test that names it and asserts its outcome;
   - a bug's reproduction test (never an extracted ID's) fails on the base and passes on HEAD;
   - every in-scope TD ID is verified (test, bench, static), inspected (with the reason) or
     deferred to operations or the QA team (with an owner); "unknown" fails;
   - every `OBJ-n` is met, with evidence;
   - the requirement and tech design diff matches the class, plus any extracted baseline (none for
     "not satisfied"); every commit touching `{{vars.requirementsDir}}` is an acceptance or accepted CR commit;
   - regression rule: every other accepted ID of the touched features has a passing test naming it,
     or goes into the hand-off as unverified.
5. **Out of bounds.** Classify every hunk per the Scope rules and compare with the declared change
   map. Unmapped hunks, leftovers (debug output, focused or skipped tests, secrets, local paths,
   hand-edited generated files) and weakened or deleted tests (except for a retired ID) are blockers.
6. **Route and screening check.** A user-visible behavior change that no in-scope ID requires, on a
   route that skipped Behavior: ask the developer; unintended → a `FAILED` blocker (restore it),
   intended → `REQUIREMENTS_GAP`. A persistent entity changed without a data section, UI placement
   changed without a UI section, or a change contradicting an N/A screening verdict (personal data
   stored while PRIV is N/A) → `DESIGN_GAP`. Re-check the screening with the catalog in the QA checks.
7. **Code review** (box 2). Read every changed file, tests and configuration included, against the
   checklist in the QA checks: correctness, spec fit, tests, security, performance,
   maintainability, compatibility. Each finding gets a severity (blocker, should-fix, nit),
   `path:line` and a concrete suggestion; name the categories that had no findings.
8. **Sensitive changes** (box 3). List for human review every change touching SEC, PRIV or SAFE
   IDs, auth, secrets, payments, personal data, migrations, CI and infrastructure, new dependencies
   or public APIs{{#if vars.sensitivePaths}}, or paths matching `{{vars.sensitivePaths}}`{{/if}}.
   Ship requests the review on the pull request; you never approve these changes yourself.
9. **Leakage.** Ticket keys (`task.key` when tracked), requester names or sentences of the original
   request in requirement or tech design files changed since the base are blockers (fixed by a
   wording-only change request in implementation).
10. **Decide and present.** Apply the verdict rules of the QA checks:

    | Result | Event |
    |---|---|
    | Every box passes (human review pending still passes) | `NEXT` |
    | A blocker, or a should-fix the developer does not move to follow-ups | `FAILED` |
    | A missing business rule or glossary term, or an intended change skipped Behavior | `REQUIREMENTS_GAP` |
    | A missing technical requirement, or a screening contradiction | `DESIGN_GAP` |

    Several apply → the earliest step wins (`REQUIREMENTS_GAP`, then `DESIGN_GAP`, then `FAILED`);
    every finding still goes into the one Rework entry, tagged with the step that owns it. The same
    finding failed in two earlier rounds → propose a GAP event instead. Show the findings and the
    verdict to the developer, who may move should-fix items and nits to follow-ups; blockers are
    never waived here.
11. **Record, commit, fire.** From the [report template](references/qa-report.md): write round n in
    `## Dev QA report`, update `## Hand-off to the QA team`, and for any event other than `NEXT`
    append the Rework entry. Commit the brief (`git add {{vars.taskNotesDir}}/<task>.md`, plus
    `.df/config.json` if you saved a command) with
    `git commit -m "docs(task): Dev QA report round <n> for <task>"`, the only commit of this
    step. Then fire the event.

## Outputs

- `### Round <n>` under `## Dev QA report` in the brief, with the diff base and the verdict.
- `## Hand-off to the QA team`: unverified IDs, deferred verifications, what to test by hand,
  human review items, follow-ups and known gaps, items not available.
- For `FAILED` and the GAP events: a Rework entry with numbered findings (ID or check, observed vs
  expected, how to reproduce, owning step).
- The commit `docs(task): Dev QA report round <n> for <task>`, then the event.

## Exit criteria

- Every check of steps 3–9 ran in this round, on committed code at HEAD, including those that
  passed in earlier rounds.
- The verdict follows the rules: `NEXT` only with no blocker, no new failure, every in-scope ID
  with the evidence boxes 1 and 4 require, no unmapped hunk, sensitive items listed.
- The report, hand-off and any Rework entry are committed; the baseline worktree is removed and
  `git status` is clean.

## Pitfalls

- Never fix code, tests, requirements or the tech design here, not even a typo: report it.
- "Tests pass" is not "requirement met": read what each test asserts. A flaky test is a failure
  until explained.
- An ID search must not match a longer ID: `CHK-1.2` is not `CHK-1.20` (whole-word search).
- Never verify uncommitted work; never stash or check out old files in the main working tree:
  baselines run in the separate worktree.
- Violations in features outside the task are follow-ups in the hand-off, not failures.
- The request is not scope: a request item missing from the accepted requirements is a hand-off
  note, not a code failure.
- Do not rubber-stamp: when nothing turns up, look again at error paths, tests and security.

